  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nHot and dry conditions will exist into the weekend as a ridge of \nhigh pressure builds over the region. High temperatures during \nthis time will be in the upper 80s to 90s, with potentially a few \n100 degree readings over the plains on Saturday (medium chance). \nRelative humidity values will also be low, making it a dry heat. \nWhile no precipitation is forecast during this time, there will be\na dry cold front passage this afternoon and evening that brings \nbreezy northwesterly wind gusts around 15 to 30 mph to the area. \nThe combination of these winds, hot temperatures, and low humidity\nwill cause elevated fire weather conditions across much of the \narea this afternoon and evening, with critical fire weather \nconditions over portions of far eastern Montana into western South\nDakota and North Dakota. Winds will be lighter across the area \nFriday, though still breezy out of the west around Park, Sweet \nGrass and Wheatland Counties in Montana. Saturday into Sunday, \nbreezy northwest to northeasterly winds will spread back across \nthe area with gusts around 10 to 30 mph, strongest over south- \ncentral Montana. Once again, these winds combined with hot \ntemperatures and low relative humidity will result in periods of \nelevated fire weather conditions. Take care to not cause a spark \ntoday through this weekend.\n\nWarm temperatures continue next week with high temperatures \ngenerally in the 80s and 90s. While mostly dry conditions will \ncontinue into Monday, the chance of precipitation increases \nTuesday into the second half of the week as better atmospheric \nmoisture combine with a few passing shortwave troughs rounding the\nridge of high pressure aloft. During this time, the higher \nrelative humidity values will limit the risk of fire weather \nconditions, however, the risk of lightning starts may increase \nshould the more favorable thunderstorm pattern develop. \n\nOtherwise, hazy skies with periods of surface smoke will continue\nlate this week into next week. For today, the cold front will \nbring a period of higher surface smoke concentrations to the area \nthis morning into tonight. For the latest air quality information,\nvisit AirNow.gov. LA",
